.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
# Anchored to the root on purpose. A bare 'data/' matches any directory of
# that name at any depth, which silently swallowed web/data/ - the demo
# tileset the published viewer loads - and would have failed the Pages
# deploy on a fresh checkout.
/data/
out/harness*/
out/
runs/
models/
# jsdom for the headless page checks (scripts/check_site.js, check_app.js).
# Installed with --no-save, so there is no manifest to commit either.
node_modules/
package-lock.json
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
*.egg-info/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Study outputs. results/ is ONE delivered study: four scene folders, each
# holding rasters, a tileset and a textured mesh, plus dataset.json and the
# control arms' numbers in arms.json.
#
# Committed: dataset.json, arms.json, summary.json, the quick-look PNGs the
# site renders, every tileset.json, and - the point of the whole delivery layer -
# each scene's textured mesh. An OBJ at stride 4 is 7.2 MB of ASCII that deflates
# to 1.6 MB, so four of them cost the pack about 8 MB and the repository stops
# being a study you have to run before you can look at anything.
#
# Not committed: the GeoTIFFs (~12 MB a scene) and the tile pyramids. Both
# rebuild from the committed inputs in seconds with `traksha build`, and the
# published viewer already carries a tileset at web/data.
results/**/*.tif
results/**/*.tiff
results/**/tiles3d/tiles/
# The structural rebuild is full resolution - one vertex per pixel, about
# 135 MB a scene - because it is the detailed download, not a web asset. It is
# derived from committed inputs and rebuilds in seconds with `traksha mesh`,
# so the numbers that describe it live in the manifest and the mesh does not
# live in git. `surface.obj`, which is budgeted, still does.
results/**/mesh/structural.obj
results/**/mesh/structural.mtl
results/_delivery_work/
